### How does orTimeout work?

orTimeout completes the CompletableFuture exceptionally with a TimeoutException if it has not completed within the specified duration. This is the fail-fast approach for strict deadlines.

```java
package com.bank.timeout;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class OrTimeoutDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<String> cf = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> callCreditBureau())  // might take 30 seconds
            .orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);        // fail after 5 seconds

        try {
            String result = cf.join();
            System.out.println("Score: " + result);
        } catch (CompletionException e) {
            if (e.getCause() instanceof TimeoutException) {
                System.out.println("Credit bureau unavailable, try later");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How does completeOnTimeout work?

completeOnTimeout completes the CompletableFuture with a fallback value instead of failing. This is the graceful degradation approach.

```java
package com.bank.timeout;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CompleteOnTimeoutDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<Integer> scoreCF = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> callCreditBureau())              // might be slow
            .completeOnTimeout(500, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);       // fallback: 500

        int score = scoreCF.join();
        // Always succeeds: real score or default 500
        System.out.println("Credit score: " + score);
    }
}
```

### How do they compare?

| Feature | orTimeout | completeOnTimeout |
|---|---|---|
| On timeout | TimeoutException | Fallback value |
| Behavior | Fail fast | Graceful degradation |
| Use case | Strict deadline, reject request | Resilient display, show cached data |
| Pipeline continues? | No (exception propagates) | Yes (with fallback) |
| When to use | Payments, trades | Dashboard widgets, reports |

### Why are timeouts non-optional?

Without a timeout, a hung HTTP call blocks a thread for the TCP timeout, which can be 300 seconds. This exhausts the thread pool and cascades to all requests.

```text
WITHOUT timeout:
  External service hangs -> thread blocked 300s
  200 threads x 300s = pool exhausted in seconds
  Circuit breaker never triggers (no failure signal, just hanging)
  Cascading failure -> entire service DOWN

WITH timeout:
  5s timeout -> TimeoutException after 5s
  Thread freed -> circuit breaker counts the failure
  Circuit opens after threshold -> fast-fail in 10ms
  Service degraded but alive!
```

### Does orTimeout cancel the underlying task?

No. orTimeout completes the CompletableFuture, but the thread running the actual task may still be blocked on IO. The task continues running until the IO call finishes or the thread is interrupted. For true cancellation, combine CF-level timeout with HTTP client-level timeouts.

```java
package com.bank.timeout;

import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.time.Duration;

public class LayeredTimeoutDemo {
    // Layer 1: HTTP client timeout (true cancellation)
    private static final HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
        .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(3))
        .build();

    // Layer 2: CF timeout (pipeline protection)
    public CompletableFuture<String> fetchScore(String customerId) {
        return CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> callBureauWithHttpClient(customerId), ioPool)
            .orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

### How do you chain timeouts in a pipeline?

Each stage can have its own timeout and fallback strategy. The overall pipeline also gets a timeout.

```java
CompletableFuture<String> result = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> fetchProfile(customerId), ioPool)
    .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    .thenCompose(profile -> fetchScore(profile.getId()))
    .orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    .thenApply(score -> decide(score))
    .completeOnTimeout("PENDING", 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
    .exceptionally(ex -> "ERROR: " + ex.getMessage());
```

### What about before Java 9?

Before Java 9, you must use the blocking `get(timeout, unit)` method, which blocks the calling thread.

```java
try {
    String result = cf.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
} catch (TimeoutException e) {
    cf.cancel(true);
    return "fallback";
} catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
    return "error";
}
```

The Java 9 methods are better because they are non-blocking and compose naturally in async pipelines.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you chain orTimeout after completeOnTimeout? Yes, but it is rarely useful since completeOnTimeout already provides a fallback.
- What is the scheduler thread for timeouts? Java 9+ uses a shared `ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor` for timeout scheduling. This thread only triggers the completion, not the actual work.
- How do timeouts interact with circuit breakers? A timeout triggers a failure count in the circuit breaker. After enough failures, the circuit opens and subsequent calls fail immediately without waiting.
